{
  "image_name": "720a73b2057c5645706b18e6dbff4e93.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates electrochemical conversion of CO₂ from industrial flue gas into multicarbon products, while highlighting how trace SO₂ impurities interfere at the catalyst interface. On the left, flue gas containing CO₂ and up to about 400 ppm SO₂ is fed into a membrane-electrode-assembly electrolyzer. With KOH on one side and oxygen evolving at the other, the device converts CO₂ into products such as ethylene and ethanol.\n\nThe right side zooms into the cathode structure, showing a porous gas diffusion layer coated with catalyst particles, identified as Cu, and covered partly by a PFSA ionomer. Gas molecules diffuse through the porous region to reach the catalyst surface, where CO₂ reduction is intended to occur. However, SO₂ is also present and appears to enter the same interfacial environment. The enlarged inset suggests that SO₂ is transformed into a negatively charged sulfur-containing species, likely sulfite or sulfur trioxide-related, and that this species migrates away through the ionomer/water region.\n\nOverall, the image conveys both the promise of directly using flue-gas CO₂ in an MEA electrolyzer and the key challenge that sulfur contaminants can penetrate the catalyst layer and alter the local electrochemical environment.",
